        <h2 id="why-bubbles" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Why Rollers Cause Bubbles
        </h2>

        <p>
          Bubbles are the defining problem of rolling polyurethane, and understanding exactly where they come from tells you which applicator to buy and, just as importantly, which ones to leave on the shelf.
        </p>

        <p>
          A roller works by rotating. As the cover turns and lifts away from the surface, it pulls air into the wet film behind it. With paint that hardly matters — paint is thick, the bubbles break and the film levels before it sets. Polyurethane is thinner, cures faster and is looked at far more critically, so those bubbles are trapped as the film hardens and stay there permanently.
        </p>

        <p>
          Three variables control how bad this gets.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Cell structure.</strong> Foam rollers are the worst offenders because their open cells physically compress and release air with every rotation. This is why the applicator most often recommended to beginners is the one most likely to cause the exact defect they are worried about.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Nap length.</strong> A long nap holds a large volume of finish and deposits a thick film. Thick films trap air more readily and sag more, and both problems compound.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Speed.</strong> The most controllable factor and the one people get wrong. A roller moved quickly generates turbulence in the wet film. Moved slowly, it lays the material down instead of stirring it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two further contributors are worth naming because they have nothing to do with the roller. <strong>Shaking the can</strong> whips air into the finish before it ever reaches the surface — always stir. And <strong>applying to a cold surface</strong> slows levelling, so bubbles that would otherwise break have time to set instead.
        </p>

        <h2 id="nap" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Nap Length and Material
        </h2>

        <p>
          Two specifications are what matter here, and both of them are printed on the wrapper.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nap Length</h3>

        <p>
          For polyurethane the answer is short. <strong>1/4 inch</strong> is the default for smooth wood — floors, table tops, cabinet doors — and lays a thin, even film that levels well. <strong>3/8 inch</strong> is worth stepping up to on floors with visible grain texture or slightly open pores, where a very short nap struggles to reach into the surface.
        </p>

        <p>
          Anything longer belongs to paint. A 1/2 or 3/4 inch nap holds several times the finish and deposits a film thick enough to sag on vertical surfaces and to cure unevenly on horizontal ones.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cover Material</h3>

        <p>
          <strong>Microfiber</strong> is the sensible default. It holds finish well, releases it evenly, sheds little, and works with both oil and water-based products.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Mohair and mohair blends</strong> lay the smoothest film of anything available and are traditional for varnish and oil-based polyurethane. They hold less material and are delicate, so they suit close-viewed surfaces rather than large floors.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Woven synthetics</strong> — as distinct from knitted ones — are the low-shedding choice. Construction matters more than fibre here: knitted covers hold fibres in loops that pull free, woven covers lock them in.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Foam</strong> should be avoided for the reasons above. The one defensible use is a small flat panel with water-based finish applied very slowly, and even then a microfiber cover does the job better.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Lambswool</strong> is a floor-specific applicator for oil-based finish, usually on a block rather than a roller. It carries a lot of material and covers ground quickly, which is why it persists on large oil-based floor jobs.
        </p>


