        <h2 id="roller-vs-tbar" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Roller vs T-Bar vs Pad
        </h2>

        <p>
          For floors specifically, the roller is not automatically the right tool, and it is worth knowing what the alternatives do better.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A roller</strong> is versatile, cheap, works with any finish and any surface, and is the right answer for furniture, cabinets, doors and small floors. Its weakness is inherent: rotation introduces air.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A T-bar applicator</strong> is a flat microfiber pad on a long handle, used almost universally by professionals for water-based floor finish. Because nothing rotates, it lays the film rather than stirring it, so bubbling is essentially eliminated and film thickness is far more consistent. It is also much faster across a large floor and you work standing up. The constraint is that it is for water-based finish on flat floors only — oil-based products will destroy the pad, and it cannot cut in or handle anything three-dimensional.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A lambswool block applicator</strong> is the oil-based equivalent: a wool pad on a block and handle, which carries a large volume of finish and spreads it quickly. It is the traditional tool for oil-based floor work and still the fastest way to cover a big area with it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A brush</strong> remains necessary whatever else you use, because every floor has a perimeter and every piece of furniture has edges. Cut in with the brush first, then fill the field with the roller or pad while the cut-in is still wet so the two blend. Our guide to <a href="/best-brush-for-water-based-polyurethane/">brushes for water-based polyurethane</a> covers that side.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical recommendation: <strong>water-based floor, use a T-bar. Oil-based floor, use lambswool or a 1/4 inch microfiber roller. Furniture and cabinetry, use a short-nap roller or a brush.</strong>
        </p>

        <h2 id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Technique That Avoids Marks
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Prepare the cover.</strong> Wash a new cover, spin it dry, and remove loose fibres with tape. For water-based finish, dampen a microfiber cover slightly and spin out the excess so it loads evenly on the first pass. Never pre-dampen with water for oil-based finish.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Stir the finish, never shake it.</strong> This is worth repeating because it causes bubbles that people then blame on the roller. Stir slowly with a flat stick, lifting any settled flattening agent off the bottom.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Load evenly and remove the excess.</strong> Roll the cover through the tray and out onto the ramp several times until it is uniformly loaded but not dripping. An unevenly loaded cover deposits a heavy stripe on its first pass.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Work in manageable sections.</strong> Two to three feet wide on a floor, and always parallel to the boards. Sections that are too large mean the leading edge sets before you reach it with the next pass, which is what creates a lap mark.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Lay it down slowly.</strong> One steady pass in each direction at a speed that feels almost too slow. You are placing the finish, not spreading it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep a wet edge.</strong> Overlap each pass into the wet edge of the last. With water-based finish, which skins in minutes, this is the discipline that determines whether the floor has visible joins.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Do not go back.</strong> Once a section has begun to tack, leave it. Rolling over setting polyurethane drags the film and leaves texture that no amount of subsequent sanding fully removes.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Plan your exit.</strong> On a floor, work away from the door. It sounds obvious and it is a mistake people make once.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sand lightly between coats and remove every trace of dust.</strong> A quick pass with 320 grit knocks down raised grain and any nibs that settled into the last coat, and gives the next one something to key into. Vacuum thoroughly afterwards, then wipe with a cloth barely damp with the appropriate solvent. Dust picked up by a roller does not stay on the roller — it gets redistributed across the whole floor in the next pass, which is why a careless clean-up between coats shows up everywhere rather than in one place.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frames, Handles and Trays</h3>

        <p>
          The cover gets all the attention but the frame it sits on affects the result more than people expect. A cheap wire frame flexes under pressure, which means the cover contacts the floor unevenly and lays a film that is thicker on one side than the other. A rigid cage frame with five or more wires holds the cover round and keeps pressure even across its width.
        </p>

        <p>
          Width is worth thinking about too. A nine-inch roller is standard and right for most floors, but a six-inch or four-inch frame is far easier to control on a table top, a stair tread or a cabinet door, where a full-width roller overhangs the work and deposits finish where you did not want it. Owning one of each costs very little.
        </p>

        <p>
          An extension pole is not optional on a floor. Working from a pole rather than on your knees keeps the roller at a consistent angle and lets you see the surface in reflected light as you go, which is how you spot a missed patch or a heavy lap while it is still wet enough to fix. It also removes the temptation to lean on the roller, which is a common cause of uneven film thickness.
        </p>

        <p>
          For trays, a disposable plastic liner is worth using on every single coat without exception. Cured polyurethane in the bottom of a tray flakes off into the next batch, and picking hardened specks out of a wet floor is a job nobody should have to do twice.
        </p>

        <h2 id="care" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Cleaning and Reuse
        </h2>

        <p>
          Whether a cover is worth cleaning depends almost entirely on which finish you used.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Water-based polyurethane</strong> washes out with warm water and a little detergent, provided you do it immediately. Work the cover until the water runs clear, spin it dry — a spinner is genuinely worth owning if you do this often — and stand it on end to finish drying. A good woven cover survives several cycles this way and performs as well on the third coat as the first.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Oil-based polyurethane</strong> requires mineral spirits, several changes of it, and the result is still rarely a cover you would trust on a visible surface. Any residue that cures inside the nap will shed into the next coat as hard specks. For most people the honest answer is to treat oil-based covers as disposable and buy multi-packs.
        </p>

        <p>
          A note on storage between coats: rather than washing a cover mid-job, wrap it tightly in plastic or a sealed bag and it will stay usable for several hours, or overnight in a refrigerator. This works with both finish types and saves a cleaning cycle in the middle of a floor.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, dispose of rags and covers used with oil-based finish safely. Oil-based products cure by oxidation, which generates heat, and a bunched-up pile of oil-soaked material can genuinely self-ignite. Spread them flat outdoors to dry completely, or seal them in a metal container with water. This is a real hazard rather than a theoretical one.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a foam roller</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The most common mistake in this category. Foam cells collapse and release air into the film, so a foam roller produces exactly the bubbles people buy it to avoid.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Rolling too fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Speed whips air into the finish. Load the roller, lay the material down in a slow steady pass, and let it level rather than working it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a nap meant for paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A half-inch or three-quarter-inch nap holds far too much finish and deposits a film thick enough to sag. For polyurethane you want a short, dense nap.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the pre-wash on a new roller</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Every new cover sheds loose fibres. Washing and spinning it dry before the first use takes two minutes and keeps fibres out of the finish.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Back-rolling a surface that has started to set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Water-based polyurethane skins fast. Going back over a section that has begun to tack drags the film and leaves permanent texture.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of roller should I use for polyurethane?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A short, dense nap — 1/4 inch microfiber or mohair is ideal. These lay a thin, controlled film without holding so much finish that it sags, and without the open cell structure that releases air bubbles into the surface.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a foam roller for polyurethane?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">You can, and it is usually a mistake. Foam cells compress and release trapped air as the roller turns, which is a direct cause of bubbles in the film. Where foam works acceptably is on very small flat surfaces with water-based finish applied slowly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my polyurethane have bubbles?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Nearly always the applicator or the technique — a foam roller, a nap that is too long, rolling too quickly, or shaking the can before use. It is very rarely the finish itself. Slow down, use a short dense nap, and stir rather than shake.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a roller or a brush better for polyurethane?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A roller for large flat areas like floors and table tops, because it is faster and lays a more even film. A brush for edges, corners, detail and small pieces. Most jobs use both — cut in with the brush, fill with the roller.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What nap length for polyurethane on floors?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">1/4 inch is the standard answer, or a 3/8 inch microfiber for a floor with heavier texture. Anything longer holds too much material and deposits a film that sags and dries unevenly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a T-bar applicator?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A flat pad on a T-shaped handle, used for applying water-based floor finish. It lays a very even, controlled film across a large floor faster than a roller and with essentially no bubbling, which is why professional floor finishers use them.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you reuse a polyurethane roller?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Water-based finish washes out with water and the cover can be reused several times if you wash and spin it properly. Oil-based finish requires mineral spirits and the result is rarely worth the effort — most people treat oil-based covers as disposable.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to wet the roller before using it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Dampen a microfiber cover slightly with water before using water-based polyurethane, then spin out the excess. It helps the cover load evenly on the first pass. Never dampen a cover with water before using oil-based finish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

